# Implementation Plan: Perfectly Timed Sound Effects

The goal is to elevate the audio drama experience by injecting perfectly timed Sound Effects (SFX) that synchronize with the AI-generated dialogue, without relying on complex backend mixing software like FFmpeg.

## Proposed Changes

We will use a **Cumulative Timing Architecture** to trigger SFX in the browser.

### 1. Schema & Prompt Upgrades
#### [MODIFY] `app/schemas/script.py`
Add an optional `sfx` field to the `ScriptLine` model (e.g., "explosion", "door", "alarm", "laser").

#### [MODIFY] `app/agents/nodes.py`
Update the LangGraph Script Writer agent to occasionally attach an `sfx` to a line of dialogue when the action calls for it.

### 2. Audio Processing Upgrade
#### [NEW Dependency] `mutagen`
A pure-Python library to read the exact duration (in seconds) of MP3 files.

#### [MODIFY] `app/worker/tasks.py`
When concatenating the individual speech chunks into the final MP3:
- Calculate the duration of each chunk using `mutagen`.
- Keep a running total of the elapsed time (`current_time`).
- If a script line has an `sfx` attached, record its precise timestamp in an `sfx_schedule` array (e.g., `[{"sfx": "alarm", "time": 4.25}]`).
- Save the `sfx_schedule` to the database alongside the `script_json`.

### 3. SFX Generation
#### [MODIFY] `generate_mock_audio.py`
Synthesize simple mock SFX tracks (e.g., `alarm.wav`, `explosion.wav`, `laser.wav`, `door.wav`) to be saved in `static/sfx/`.

### 4. Live Browser Mixing
#### [MODIFY] `static/app.js`
- Create a pool of HTML5 `<audio>` elements dedicated to playing SFX.
- Read the `sfx_schedule` from the completed job data.
- Attach an `ontimeupdate` event listener to the main voice player. As the playback time progresses, it will trigger the correct SFX exactly when the timestamp is reached.

## User Review Required

> [!IMPORTANT]
